  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After accepting Eleonora's offer, Chef Barinov returns home to celebrate the news with Elena, who has an exciting professional announcement of her own. Listen to the full conversation in Russian first, and then we will walk through every line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сегодня я получил большое повышение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "И что ты им ответил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты сама как думаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Думаю, что ресторан приобрёл владельца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Точнее, ресторан приобрёл нового совладельца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Какая же это всё-таки красота!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Мне предложили вести колонку в журнале.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Надо писать о ресторанах Москвы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Им очень понравилась моя книга.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебя от души поздравляю!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе большое, мой дорогой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Правда, я уже им отказалась.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сегодня я получил большое повышение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Today I received a big promotion.' Notice how Russian expresses 'tonight' and 'promotion' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Elena answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "И что ты им ответил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what did you answer them?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'and' and finishing on 'answered'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты сама как думаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what do you think yourself?' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'think' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Думаю, что ресторан приобрёл владельца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I think that the restaurant gained an owner.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'acquired' places the action in the masculine past tense. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Точнее, ресторан приобрёл нового совладельца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'More accurately, the restaurant gained a new co-owner.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'definitely' and 'restaurant' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Какая же это всё-таки красота!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How wonderful all of this is!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'what a' before landing on 'beauty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Мне предложили вести колонку в журнале.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They offered me a column in a magazine.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'to me' and finishing on 'log'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Надо писать о ресторанах Москвы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I would write about the restaurants of Moscow.' Elena anchors the line on 'need', while 'Moscow' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Им очень понравилась моя книга.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They liked my book very much.' In Russian, the idea of 'them' flows naturally into 'book' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебя от души поздравляю!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I congratulate you from the bottom of my heart!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'congratulations'. Elena answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Спасибо тебе большое, мой дорогой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you very much, my dear.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'thank you' before landing on 'expensive'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Правда, я уже им отказалась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Though I already turned them down.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'really' and finishing on 'refused'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
